🚀 Feature

Migrate profiling framework to use timemory as the backend to build the profiling framework. Timemory is essentially a C++ toolkit for building performance analysis and logging interfaces. Migrating the profiling framework internally could maintain the current Python and C++ APIs but new capabilities could be trivially added and the internal implementation would be significantly simplified.
Motivation

On the PyTorch end: easier to add new profiling features, more modular implementation, full support for exporting results to JSON/XML, easier to add python bindings to profiling tools (timemory example and source), easier to combine results between threads and MPI ranks, and support for profiling analysis in pandas all while maintaining backwards-compatibility. On my end: demonstration of how this framework can be used to create a custom profiling API in a popular framework.
Pitch

As I've mentioned previously, you can keep any/all of the current interface. In my experience, it's generally pretty straight-forward to wrap existing infrastructure into a component and there is no real difference between a timemory-provided component and one that the user (i.e. PyTorch) defines. The framework is designed such that you have full control over what data is collected, how it is collected, and how it is stored. There are no strong requirements to use any specific part of the library. There really isn't a downside but the benefits are pretty high with the aforementioned benefits in Motivation and there is a built-in, customizable empirical roofline toolkit for estimating the peak performance on the machine among other things.
As far as doing the actual implementation, I will certainly assist with the initial implementation but I don't aim to be the maintainer. It appears that @malfet and @ilia-cher have been working on the new interface based on git blame. Does using this toolkit interest y'all?
Alternatives

To my knowledge, CBTF is basically the only framework that I've found that attempts to provide something analogous to what timemory does. Of course, you could continue as is but I think you'll find the implementation with timemory far more maintainable.
Additional context

Timemory: Modular Performance Analysis for HPC (paper)
Timemory Presentation at ISC'20 (video)
Here is the gist of the design:

Four main categories to the interface:

Components: encapsulate how to perform some action or measurement and/or store data for one measurement

In general, a component defines it's own interface and everything else adapts to it
May be used directly or in conjunction with bundlers


Bundlers: holds a tuple of components and whose member functions act as common interface for invoking the component member functions
Operations: used by bundlers to query the existence of member functions and compatible overloads for each component
Storage: (optional) persistent call-stack tracking supporting hierarchical profiles, flat profiles, hierarchical timeline profiles, flat timeline profiles

It is perfectly valid to maintain a map/vector of bundles and do your call-stack tracking





Here is a sample using timemory in lieu of raw measurements for context. The overhead in this case is identical (trivial in both cases):
using bundle_t = tim::lightweight_tuple<wall_clock, tim::quirk::config<tim::quirk::auto_start>>;
static bundle_t single_sum{ "single" };

int64_t
tim_fibonacci(int64_t n)
{
    bundle_t _scoped{};
    auto _result = (n < 2) ? n : (tim_fibonacci(n - 1) + tim_fibonacci(n - 2));
    single_sum += _scoped.stop();
    return _result;
}

static double nseconds = 0;

int64_t
raw_fibonacci(int64_t n)
{
    auto _start  = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();
    auto _result = (n < 2) ? n : (raw_fibonacci(n - 1) + raw_fibonacci(n - 2));
    nseconds += std::chrono::duration<double>{ std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now() - _start }.count();
    return _result;
}
You can verify it with this mini-replica of timemory on godbolt demonstrating the above:

